  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**:\n   - **Central Idea**: We employ a multi-resolution population-based optimization algorithm using Bayesian optimization to systematically explore the parameter space of \\(h(x)\\). The approach involves maintaining multiple candidate solutions at varying resolutions (from 320 to higher, up to 512 points) and using Bayesian optimization with a probabilistic surrogate model to guide the search. We also incorporate a coordinate descent step to refine solutions found by the global optimizer.\n   - **Why Different**: This differs from the previous SLSQP-based local search by using Bayesian optimization which provides a global search view, handles the non-convex constraints better, and automatically dedicates more resources to promising regions. It addresses local optima by maintaining a population and transferable solutions across resolutions.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**:\n   - **Variables**: The function \\(h(x)\\) is represented as an \\(n\\)-point piecewise-constant approximation on \\([0, 2]\\) with \\(1 \\leq n \\leq 512\\).\n   - **Objective**: \\(\\min_{n,h} \\max_k \\left( \\iint } % {\\backslash \\mathrm{\\int } h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \\right) \\cdot \\Delta x\\).\n   - **Constraints**: \n     - \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) for all \\(i\\)\n     - \\(\\sum h_i \\cdot \\Delta x = 1\\)\n   - **Representation**: Uses \\(n\\) equidistant points. The parent solution (320 points) is interpolated for lower resolutions, and upscaled with linear interpolation for higher resolutions.\n   - **Surrogate Formulation**: An Expected Improvement process from Bayesian optimization is used to balance exploration and exploitation. The surrogate is created via a Radial Basis Function (RBF) network.\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**:\n   - **Preprocessing**: \n     - Validate the incoming `initial_h_values` and make them feasible.\n     - `n_max = 512`, `n_min = 320`\n   - **Coordinate Handling**:\n     - Apply the provided `upsample_h` function for interpolation between different points.\n     - Ensure interpolation maintains box constraints and the equality constraint \\(\\sum h_i \\cdot \\Delta x = 1\\).\n   - **Solver Structure**:\n     1. Sample the initial population uniformly from [0, 1] adjusted to satisfy the sum.\n     2. Initialize a surrogate model with an RBF network.\n     3. Perform a loop:\n        a. Select the next point via Expected Improvement.\n        b. Evaluate at the next point: compute C5 using `np.correlate` with `mode='full'`, clip the result, and scale by dx.\n        c. Update the surrogate and coarser scales with the new solution.\n        d. After completion, perform coordinate descent to refine the solution.\n   - **Termination**:\n     - Break when budget exceeded or convergence criteria met.\n     - Final solution must be feasible and satisfy constraints.\n\n4. **Search Choices**:\n   - **Surrogate Tuning**: RBF width parameter set to the initial population size (10) initially.\n   - **Hyperparameters**: Learning rate decay upon backtracking, penalty factor for constraint violation.\n   - **Resolution**: Multi-resolution exploration (320 to 512 points).\n   - **Restarting**: When surrogate predicts \\(\\delta\\)-transient improvement, restart the RBF network at current scale.\n\n5. **Compute Budget**:\n   - Target between 500 and 3000 function evaluations, using adaptive time allocation: 60% to scaling up the best solution, 30% to population evolution, 10% to coordinate descent.\n   - Check budget in the runtime loop before evaluating the next point.\n\n6. **Validation**:\n   - Verify that returned \\(h\\) meets \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\), \\(\\sum h_i = n/2\\).\n   - Double-check computation of C5 using the correlation method.\n   - Output the best improvement by the algorithm.",
